      "t": "Tuesday sync notes",
      "d": "Decisions\n- Ship the glasses simulator this week; real Bluetooth link next week.\n- The assistant stays the single entry point for personal tasks.\n- Notifications never interrupt; The classifier only decides notify or suppress.\n\nOwners\n- Robin: control map, page layouts.\n- Hermes: morning report cards as JSON.\n\nOpen questions\n- Memo upload path: share intent vs manual.\n- Classifier API shape and override rules.\n- Which font reads best at 540 by 220.\n\nNext sync Thursday 4 PM."
    },
    {
      "t": "Waveguide displays explainer",
      "d": "A diffractive waveguide carries light from a tiny projector at the temple, through the lens, and out toward your eye. Gratings etched into the glass bend the light in and then out again.\n\nBecause the light is spread across a small eye box, brightness drops quickly as the field of view grows. That is why small heads-up displays like the iO can hit over 1,000 nits while wide AR displays struggle outdoors.\n\nMonochrome green is common for a reason: green microLEDs are the most efficient, and the eye is most sensitive to green, so the same power looks brighter.\n\nThe trade-off is that colour, fine detail and large text areas all cost brightness or battery. Designing for these displays means short text, high contrast, and big clear shapes."
    },
    {
      "t": "Q4 usage report",
      "d": "Total agent hours this quarter rose 38% over Q3. Claude Code accounts for most long-running threads; Codex handles most quick fixes.\n\nPeak usage lands on Monday and Tuesday mornings. Weekly limits were hit twice, both in the last week of October."
    }
  ],
